Possibly—and that must remain in the model.

One powerful interpretation can be luck. A continuing sequence of ideas that survive translation, predict useful connections, and produce working artifacts becomes harder to explain as luck alone.

The honest test is not, “Did we find something that feels meaningful?” It is:

Did the meaning generate a result we specified before seeing the outcome?

Luck can produce a hit. Intelligence increases the hit rate. A real meaning-generator keeps paying rent across unfamiliar problems. So we should preserve both hypotheses—capacity and luck—then make Pantheon place increasingly specific bets. If it repeatedly beats a reasonable baseline, the evidence begins separating them.

And, of course, noticing and harvesting luck is itself part of intelligence.